+# SPEC: Renewals
+
+## Why
+
+Members ask to keep books longer. Today staff do it by returning the copy and checking it out
+again, which breaks the loan history: one borrowing shows up as several loans. A renewal extends
+the loan the member already has, so the history stays one loan, and the rules that protect other
+members (nobody waiting, nothing owed) and the library (overdue days are still fined) still apply.
+
+Earlier specs: `docs/specs/overdue-fines.md`, `docs/specs/holds.md`. Rules: `docs/adr/0005`
+(renewing overdue loans), `0007` (who may renew), `0008` (renewal terms, fine cap). Amounts are in
+euros (€); the code only sees `Decimal`.
+
+## Interface (fixed by the owner; other code calls exactly this)
+
+- `LendingService.renew(loan_id: str) -> Loan` in `src/lending/service.py` — renews the open loan in
+  place (same `loan_id`) and returns it.
+- `Loan` in `src/lending/models.py` gets a field `renewals: int` (default `0`): how many times the
+  loan has been renewed.
+- Add `RenewalRefused(LendingError)` to `src/lending/errors.py` for renewals that can't be granted;
+  unknown loan ids keep raising `LoanNotFound`, and keep using the other existing error classes where
+  they already fit.
+
+Settled with the owner (ADR 0007, 0008): the paid-up rule raises the existing `AccountBlocked`.
+
+## Terms
+
+- **Renewal**: giving an open book loan a fresh full loan period counted from the renewal day.
+- **Renewal limit**: 2 renewals per loan.
+- **Renewal window**: from 5 calendar days before the loan's due date onwards, overdue included.
+- **Paid up**: a balance of exactly €0.00.
+- **Waiting hold**: an active hold with no copy set aside yet (ADR 0006).
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+### A successful renewal
+- R1. `renew(loan_id)` returns the same loan (same `loan_id`), still open, with `renewals` one higher
+  than before.
+- R2. The new `due_on` is what a checkout of that copy made on the renewal day would get: 21 days
+  after the renewal day for a book, moved to the Monday if it lands on a Sunday. Example: a book due
+  Mon 23 March 2026 and renewed Thu 19 March 2026 is due Thu 9 April 2026.
+- R3. Renewing changes nothing else about the loan or the copy: `borrowed_on`, `copy_id` and
+  `member_id` stay the same, the copy stays `ON_LOAN`, and `loan_history` and `open_loans` show the
+  same single loan (no new loan is created).
+- R4. `get_loan(loan_id)` afterwards shows the renewed loan (new `due_on`, new `renewals`).
+- R5. A loan that has never been renewed has `renewals == 0`.
+
+### Limit and window
+- L1. A loan can be renewed twice. A third renewal raises `RenewalRefused`.
+- L2. A loan is renewable from 5 calendar days before its due date onwards, including the due date
+  itself and any day after it. Example: due Mon 23 March 2026 → renewable on Wed 18 March 2026,
+  refused with `RenewalRefused` on Tue 17 March 2026.
+- L3. The window follows the current due date: right after a renewal (same day or the next), the
+  loan cannot be renewed again (`RenewalRefused`); once inside the window of the new due date, it can.
+
+### Refusals
+- X1. An unknown loan id raises `LoanNotFound`.
+- X2. A loan that has already been returned raises `RenewalRefused`.
+- X3. A DVD loan raises `RenewalRefused`, whatever its dates. Books can be renewed.
+- X4. A loan whose title has any waiting hold (by any member) raises `RenewalRefused`.
+- X5. Ready holds do not block a renewal, nor do fulfilled, cancelled or expired holds.
+- X6. A member whose balance is above €0.00 — even €0.01 — gets `AccountBlocked`, carrying the
+  member, their balance and a bar of €0.00. A member with a balance of exactly €0.00 can renew, so
+  paying the balance off lifts the refusal.
+- X7. A refused renewal changes nothing: the loan's `due_on` and `renewals`, the copy, the holds and
+  the member's balance are as they were, and no account entry is added.
+- X8. Children renew under the same rules as adults.
+- X9. All new errors are `LendingError`s. When several refusals apply at once, which one is raised is
+  not specified; one of them is.
+
+### Overdue loans
+- O1. Renewing an overdue loan of an adult charges the late fine accrued up to and including the
+  renewal day, counted exactly as if the copy had been returned that day (late days, rates, child
+  exemption from `docs/specs/overdue-fines.md`). It is one `fine` account entry, linked to the loan.
+  Example: a book due Mon 23 March 2026 renewed Mon 30 March 2026 charges €1.50 and is due
+  Mon 20 April 2026.
+- O2. Renewing a loan that is not overdue (renewed on or before its due date) charges nothing.
+- O3. After a renewal, lateness is counted against the new due date only: every late day is fined
+  exactly once. A copy returned on or before the new due date charges nothing more; a copy returned
+  late after a renewal is fined only for the days after the new due date.
+- O4. After an overdue renewal the member owes the fine, so a further renewal is refused with
+  `AccountBlocked` until it is paid (X6).
+- O5. A child's overdue loan renews without any charge.
+
+### Fine cap across the loan
+- C1. All fines on one loan together — renewal fines and the return fine — never exceed the title's
+  replacement cost. A fine that would take the total past it is reduced to what is left.
+- C2. Once a loan's fines have reached the replacement cost, a later renewal or return of that loan
+  charges nothing more.
+- C3. A loan that is never renewed is fined exactly as before.
+
+### Unchanged
+- U1. All existing behaviour and tests keep passing.
+
+## Non-goals
+
+- A "last renewed" date or a renewal log on the loan; `renewals` is the only count kept.
+- Renewing DVDs, or different renewal limits or windows per material or member category.
+- Staff overrides that renew past the limit, the window, a waiting hold or an unpaid balance.
+- Automatic renewals, or notifying members that a loan is due or was renewed.
+- Waiving or refunding the fine charged at renewal.
+- Changing how holds, checkout, returns or the overdue report work.
+- Persistence beyond the in-memory repository.
