+# 0002: How overdue fines are counted and charged
+
+## Context
+
+Late returns cost nothing, so DVDs come back weeks late. The owner set the rules.
+
+## Decision
+
+- **Late days** are the days after the due date up to and including the return
+  (or reckoning) day, excluding days the library is closed (`policy.CLOSED_WEEKDAYS`,
+  currently Sunday). Nobody can return a copy on a closed day, so it is not counted.
+  Example: due Mon 23 March, back Mon 30 March → 6 late days.
+- **Rates** per late day: books 0.25, DVDs 1.00 (`policy.FINE_PER_LATE_DAY`).
+- **Cap**: the fine for one loan never exceeds the title's replacement cost.
+- **Children are never fined.** Their late returns are processed normally and
+  nothing is charged. (Manual staff charges still apply to children.)
+- Fines are charged **only when the copy comes back**. One fine entry per late
+  return; nothing accrues on the balance while a copy is still out.
+- The calculation is one function (`policy.overdue_fine`) taking the loan's due
+  date and a reckoning day, so renewals (ADR 0005) reuse exactly the same rules.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- A member with a copy months overdue but not yet returned is not blocked by it.
+  Accrual is a deliberate non-goal for now.
+- Changing a rate or adding a closed day changes only `lending.policy`.
diff --git a/docs/adr/0003-account-blocks.md b/docs/adr/0003-account-blocks.md
