+# 0005: Renewing an overdue loan charges the fine so far (for later)
+
+## Context
+
+Renewals will come after holds. If renewing a late loan simply moved the due date,
+the lateness before the renewal would never be fined. The owner stated this rule in
+advance.
+
+## Decision
+
+- Renewing a loan that is already overdue charges the late fine accrued so far as
+  part of the renewal. It is counted exactly as if the copy had been returned on
+  the renewal day: same late-day counting, rates, cap and child exemption
+  (ADR 0002, `policy.overdue_fine` with the renewal day as reckoning day).
+- After the renewal, lateness is counted against the new due date.
+- Lateness before a renewal must never disappear: every late day is fined exactly
+  once, either at a renewal or at the final return.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The fine entry for a renewal is linked to the loan like a return fine (ADR 0001).
+- **Open question for the owner when renewals are built:** is the replacement-cost
+  cap per charge, or across all fines on the same loan (renewal fines plus the
+  return fine)? Today a loan has at most one fine, so the two readings agree.
+  *Answered in ADR 0008: across all fines on the same loan.*
