@@ -0,0 +1,28 @@
+# 0007: Who may renew a loan (for later)
+
+## Context
+
+Renewals are next after holds. A renewal keeps a copy away from everyone else for
+longer, so the owner set who may renew ahead of the feature being built. ADR 0005
+already fixes what a renewal of an overdue loan charges.
+
+## Decision
+
+- **Queue rule**: a loan cannot be renewed while the title has any *waiting* hold
+  (ADR 0006). Ready holds do not count, because that person already has their copy on
+  the hold shelf.
+- **Paid-up rule**: only a member whose balance is exactly 0.00 when they ask may renew.
+  Anything owed at all, however small, refuses the renewal until they have paid.
+- Both are checked before anything is charged or changed. A refused renewal leaves the
+  loan, its due date and the balance as they were.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The paid-up rule is stricter than any bar in ADR 0003 and is not a "balance ≥ bar"
+  check: it refuses when balance > 0. Implementing it as a bar of 0.00 with the usual
+  comparison would block everyone.
+- With ADR 0002 (no accrual) and ADR 0005: a paid-up member whose loan is overdue may
+  renew. The fine so far is charged by the renewal itself, so they leave owing it, and
+  they cannot renew again until it is paid.
+- The error class for each refusal is decided when renewals are built.
+  `AccountBlocked` fits the paid-up rule. *Decided in ADR 0008.*
diff --git a/docs/adr/0008-renewal-terms.md b/docs/adr/0008-renewal-terms.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..be48817
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/adr/0008-renewal-terms.md
@@ -0,0 +1,40 @@
+# 0008: Renewal terms, limits and the loan-wide fine cap
+
+## Context
+
+Members ask to keep books longer, and staff fake it by returning the copy and checking
+it out again, which breaks the loan history. ADR 0005 fixed what renewing an overdue
+loan charges and left the fine cap open; ADR 0007 fixed who may renew and left the
+error classes open. The owner settled the rest when renewals were built.
+
+## Decision
+
+- A renewal extends the **same loan** in place (same `loan_id`) and counts it in
+  `Loan.renewals`. It never closes the loan or opens a new one.
+- **New due date**: a fresh full loan period counted from the renewal day, exactly
+  like a checkout made that day (`policy.due_date(material, renewal day)`). Book due
+  Mon 23 March, renewed Thu 19 March → due Thu 9 April.
+- **Renewal limit**: at most 2 renewals per loan (`policy.MAX_RENEWALS`).
+- **Renewal window**: a loan can be renewed from 5 calendar days before its due date
+  onwards, overdue included (`policy.RENEW_WINDOW_DAYS`). Due Mon 23 March →
+  renewable from Wed 18 March.
+- **Only books can be renewed** (`policy.RENEWABLE_MATERIALS`). DVDs cannot.
+- Children follow the same rules as adults (and are never fined, ADR 0002).
+- **Fine cap is per loan** (answers ADR 0005's open question): all fines on one loan
+  together — renewal fines plus the return fine — never exceed the title's
+  replacement cost. A fine that would pass the cap is cut to what is left; once the
+  cap is reached, nothing more is charged for that loan.
+- **Errors**: an unknown loan id raises `LoanNotFound`. The paid-up rule (ADR 0007)
+  raises `AccountBlocked` with a bar of 0.00 (`policy.RENEW_MAX_BALANCE`), refusing
+  when balance > bar. Every other refusal — loan already returned, DVD, renewal limit
+  reached, outside the window, a waiting hold on the title — raises `RenewalRefused`.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Renewing the day after a renewal is refused: the new due date is a full period
+  away, so the loan is outside the window again.
+- `policy.overdue_fine` takes what the loan has already been fined, so the cap is
+  enforced in the one fine function for returns and renewals alike.
+- A loan that is never renewed has at most one fine, so its fines are unchanged.
+- No "last renewed" date is kept; `renewals` is the only record on the loan. The
+  renewal fine entry (dated, linked to the loan) is the trace for overdue renewals.
